# Credentials for the live provider tests. Copy to `.env` at the workspace root — one level
# above this checkout — and fill in. One central file serves this checkout's demos and tests and
# the sibling product checkout.
#
#   cp .env.example ../.env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Every live test skips itself with a stated reason when its variables are
# missing, so a plain `dotnet test` stays offline, free, and green without this file. A real
# environment variable always overrides the file.

# ── Amazon Bedrock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Region the model below is enabled in. This is not cosmetic: it forms part of the SigV4
# credential scope, so a mismatch is a 403 rather than a "model not found".
BEDROCK_REGION=us-west-2

# A model reachable over the OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions API — not Nova or Titan, which
# speak only Bedrock's native Converse API. See docs/guides/21-enterprise-clouds.md.
# Alternatives: openai.gpt-oss-120b-1:0, qwen.qwen3-32b-v1:0, deepseek.v3-v1:0
# Confirm what your account can actually reach:
#   aws bedrock list-foundation-models --region $BEDROCK_REGION --output text \
#     --query "modelSummaries[].modelId"
BEDROCK_CHAT_MODEL_ID=openai.gpt-oss-20b-1:0

# No Claude entry: the live tests deliberately exercise non-Anthropic models only. Invoking Claude
# on Bedrock needs a one-time use-case submission per AWS account before any request succeeds, which
# a test suite cannot perform. See BedrockAgentModel.CreateMessages.

# Bedrock API key, for the bearer-token path.
AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=

# IAM credentials, for the SigV4 path. Leave blank to skip that test; the standard AWS credential
# chain (profile, SSO, instance metadata) is used when these are set, so a profile works too.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Optional, for temporary credentials from SSO or an assumed role.
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

# ── Microsoft Foundry (formerly Azure AI Foundry / Azure OpenAI) ─────
# No extra package: Foundry's /openai/v1/ route is OpenAI-shaped and implicitly versioned, so the
# OpenAI adapter reaches it with a base-URL override alone.
#
# The portal shows one of two endpoint forms depending on the resource's vintage; append `openai/v1/`
# to whichever you get. A trailing slash is added automatically if you omit it.
#   https://<resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/
#   https://<resource>.services.ai.azure.com/openai/v1/
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://<resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/

# The *deployment name* you chose, not a model id — Foundry addresses models by deployment.
# Avoid naming a deployment after a real model id: a literal like "gpt-4o" in your code matches a
# deprecated entry in model-lifecycle.json and ANNKE002 will flag it, advising a replacement that is
# wrong, because a deployment name may front any model. See docs/guides/21-enterprise-clouds.md.
AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT=ananke-test

# Either key from the resource; they are interchangeable.
AZURE_INFERENCE_CREDENTIAL=

# Entra ID (keyless) is postponed — it would pull Azure.Identity into the tree, which nothing here
# references today. The credential seam it would use is covered offline by OpenAICredentialSeamTests.

# ── Google Gemini (Developer API) ────────────────────────────────────
# A key from aistudio.google.com. No billing account, no API enablement, no service account — which
# is why the live tests use this surface rather than Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# Check AI Studio's model list before pasting: Google's lineup churns faster than the others, and a
# stale id is the most likely reason this test fails.
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) is not covered by the live tests. It shares
# this adapter and differs only in credentials — Application Default Credentials rather than a key —
# so it needs a billing-enabled GCP project, the Vertex AI API enabled, and roles/aiplatform.user.
# Note GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS is read from the process environment by Google's auth library,
# not from this file.

# ── A self-hosted, OpenAI-compatible model server ────────────────────
# Ollama, llama-server (llama.cpp), vLLM, LM Studio. Unlike every other tier here, this one costs
# nothing and needs no account — which is why it has its own category and why it is the one most
# worth running:
#
#   dotnet test src/Ananke.slnx --filter TestCategory=LocalModel
#
# "Local" means self-hosted, not necessarily on this machine. Inference is memory-hungry and the box
# running the tests is often not the box with the RAM, so point this wherever the server actually
# is. Leave it unset to use http://localhost:11434/v1. Include the /v1 suffix: these servers expose
# their OpenAI-compatible routes underneath it.
LOCAL_MODEL_ENDPOINT=

# A model the server has already pulled — `ollama list` shows yours. Small is the point: a 1B model
# is the sharpest test of what does and does not survive at the small end, and the tool-calling and
# structured-output tests report what your deployment actually does rather than assuming.
LOCAL_MODEL_ID=llama3.2:1b

# Only needed when a gateway or proxy in front of the server checks credentials. A bare Ollama or
# vLLM ignores it, and the tests send a readable placeholder in that case.
LOCAL_MODEL_API_KEY=
